1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,891
By the end of this guide you will know how to rotate and flip your inline speed wheels so the whole set lasts far longer.

2
00:00:07,891 --> 00:00:15,718
You will also learn how often to do it, how to handle three wheel and Hi Lo frames, and when a wheel is really finished.

3
00:00:15,718 --> 00:00:20,023
Race wheels are one of the biggest running costs in speed skating.

4
00:00:20,023 --> 00:00:23,545
Most skaters wear theirs out long before they need to.

5
00:00:23,545 --> 00:00:26,675
The problem is almost never the urethane itself.

6
00:00:26,675 --> 00:00:27,849
It is uneven wear.

7
00:00:27,849 --> 00:00:30,458
The good news is that the fix is simple.

8
00:00:30,458 --> 00:00:35,871
Ten minutes after a session, once a week, keeps your wheels rolling round and even.

9
00:00:36,572 --> 00:00:40,250
When you skate, you almost never load a wheel dead flat.

10
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:41,826
You push to the outside.

11
00:00:41,826 --> 00:00:43,271
You lean into corners.

12
00:00:43,271 --> 00:00:46,686
And your body has a stronger side and a weaker side.

13
00:00:46,686 --> 00:00:50,299
Over time, this grinds the urethane into a slight cone.

14
00:00:50,299 --> 00:00:54,765
The inner edge wears lower than the outer edge, as you can see here.

15
00:00:54,765 --> 00:00:57,852
A coned wheel has less contact with the ground.

16
00:00:57,852 --> 00:01:02,253
It feels skittish, and it loses grip exactly when you need it most.

17
00:01:02,253 --> 00:01:08,164
Left alone, one or two wheels wear out while the rest of the set still has plenty of life.

18
00:01:08,164 --> 00:01:11,054
Then you end up throwing away good urethane.

19
00:01:11,754 --> 00:01:14,132
There is no single perfect number.

20
00:01:14,132 --> 00:01:22,596
A good rule for most speed skaters is to rotate every one to three sessions, depending on how hard and how far you skate.

21
00:01:22,596 --> 00:01:26,933
If you train daily on rough outdoor tarmac, rotate more often.

22
00:01:26,933 --> 00:01:32,529
If you skate a few times a week on smooth surfaces, once a week is usually fine.

23
00:01:32,529 --> 00:01:34,137
The key is consistency.

24
00:01:34,137 --> 00:01:37,635
A small rotation done regularly keeps wheels even.

25
00:01:37,635 --> 00:01:42,531
A big rotation done rarely cannot undo damage that has already set in.

26
00:01:42,531 --> 00:01:48,896
After every session, run a finger across each wheel, from the inner edge to the outer edge.

27
00:01:48,896 --> 00:01:53,163
If you feel a clear slope, that wheel needs to move and flip.

28
00:01:53,163 --> 00:01:57,010
Your fingers will notice this long before your eyes do.

29
00:01:57,710 --> 00:02:01,561
On a standard four wheel frame, you do two things at once.

30
00:02:01,561 --> 00:02:08,466
You move each wheel to a new position, and you flip it so the worn low edge becomes the fresh high edge.

31
00:02:08,466 --> 00:02:14,906
First, take all the wheels off and lay them out in order on a towel, left skate then right skate.

32
00:02:14,906 --> 00:02:19,022
Second, flip every wheel so the worn inner edge now faces out.

33
00:02:19,022 --> 00:02:21,213
This alone does most of the work.

34
00:02:21,213 --> 00:02:29,114
Third, move the most worn wheels, usually the ones under your strongest push, to the positions that see the least wear.

35
00:02:29,114 --> 00:02:33,495
Fourth, move the least worn wheels into the hardest working spots.

36
00:02:33,495 --> 00:02:36,948
Finally, refit them and check each one spins freely.

37
00:02:36,948 --> 00:02:39,604
Keep your axles snug, not overtightened.

38
00:02:39,604 --> 00:02:44,118
An axle done up too hard squeezes the bearings and robs you of roll.

39
00:02:44,818 --> 00:02:46,642
This diagram shows the idea.

40
00:02:46,642 --> 00:02:52,893
Each wheel moves to a new position, and each one is turned over, so it now wears its other edge.

41
00:02:52,893 --> 00:02:56,736
Many skaters use a front to back swap combined with a flip.

42
00:02:56,736 --> 00:03:01,034
The front wheel moves toward the rear, and every wheel turns over.

43
00:03:01,034 --> 00:03:06,439
The exact pattern matters far less than doing it consistently, and always flipping.

44
00:03:06,439 --> 00:03:15,296
So keep your routine simple enough that you will actually stick to it after a hard session, when you are tired and just want to go home.

45
00:03:15,996 --> 00:03:18,586
Not every skater runs four equal wheels.

46
00:03:18,586 --> 00:03:26,097
On a three wheel frame there are only three positions per foot, so every wheel works harder and wear arrives faster.

47
00:03:26,097 --> 00:03:33,091
The middle wheel usually takes the most load, because it sits under your body weight for most of the stroke.

48
00:03:33,091 --> 00:03:34,710
A simple loop works well.

49
00:03:34,710 --> 00:03:42,480
Move the middle wheel to the front, the front wheel to the rear, and the rear wheel into the middle, flipping all three.

50
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:50,186
Swapping wheels between your left and right skate helps too, because your two legs almost never load them the same way.

51
00:03:50,186 --> 00:03:52,453
A Hi Lo frame needs one extra rule.

52
00:03:52,453 --> 00:03:57,892
The smaller wheel cannot swap into a big wheel position without changing your pitch.

53
00:03:57,892 --> 00:04:03,461
So rotate the three larger wheels among themselves, and flip the small wheel in place.

54
00:04:04,161 --> 00:04:07,255
Wheels are sold as a set of eight for a reason.

55
00:04:07,255 --> 00:04:16,408
A full set covers a pair of four wheel frames, keeps the compound identical in every position, and starts every wheel at the same diameter.

56
00:04:16,408 --> 00:04:19,502
That matched start is what makes rotation work.

57
00:04:19,502 --> 00:04:25,494
You can move any wheel to any position without changing how the frame sits under your foot.

58
00:04:25,494 --> 00:04:27,206
Compound matters here too.

59
00:04:27,206 --> 00:04:34,449
If you mix a firmer wheel into a softer set, the harder wheel will sit slightly proud as the others wear down.

60
00:04:34,449 --> 00:04:40,572
It takes more than its share of the load, and that is the fastest way to cone a single wheel.

61
00:04:40,572 --> 00:04:44,918
Keep the set together, rotate it together, and retire it together.

62
00:04:45,618 --> 00:04:52,263
Every rotation is the perfect moment to look after your bearings, because the wheels are already off.

63
00:04:52,263 --> 00:04:56,670
A pair of four wheel skates runs sixteen 608 bearings plus spacers.

64
00:04:56,670 --> 00:05:02,394
They all wear at different rates, depending on position and how much grit they pick up.

65
00:05:02,394 --> 00:05:04,367
Wipe the grit off the shields.

66
00:05:04,367 --> 00:05:07,130
Spin each bearing and listen for grinding.

67
00:05:07,130 --> 00:05:10,749
If any sound dry, add a drop of proper skate lubricant.

68
00:05:10,749 --> 00:05:17,854
A light, purpose made oil keeps them spinning freely without pulling in the dust that thick greases attract.

69
00:05:17,854 --> 00:05:24,038
Doing the wheel rotation and a quick bearing check as one routine keeps your whole setup fast.

70
00:05:24,038 --> 00:05:28,774
You also catch small problems before they cost you a wheel or a bearing.

71
00:05:29,474 --> 00:05:35,684
Larger wheels, such as 110 millimetre, carry more speed, so even wear matters even more.

72
00:05:35,684 --> 00:05:41,752
On a set of tall wheels, one badly coned wheel throws off the feel of the whole frame.

73
00:05:41,752 --> 00:05:46,480
You notice it as a wobble, or a loss of confidence in fast corners.

74
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:47,891
Storage matters too.

75
00:05:47,891 --> 00:05:51,772
Urethane does not like long spells of heat or sunlight.

76
00:05:51,772 --> 00:05:56,430
Do not leave your skates baking in a car boot or on a hot balcony.

77
00:05:56,430 --> 00:06:02,498
In an Indian summer, a set left in a parked car can be quietly ruined in an afternoon.

78
00:06:02,498 --> 00:06:09,343
After skating outdoors, wipe road grime off with a dry cloth, so grit stays out of your bearings.

79
00:06:09,343 --> 00:06:13,012
Avoid harsh solvents, which can attack the urethane.

80
00:06:13,012 --> 00:06:17,034
Store your skates somewhere cool and dry, off the ground.

81
00:06:17,734 --> 00:06:19,830
So when is a wheel really done?

82
00:06:19,830 --> 00:06:21,318
There are three signs.

83
00:06:21,318 --> 00:06:23,617
It has worn down close to the hub.

84
00:06:23,617 --> 00:06:28,823
It has coned so badly that flipping no longer gives you a usable riding edge.

85
00:06:28,823 --> 00:06:33,691
Or the urethane has gone hard and lifeless with age and no longer grips.

86
00:06:33,691 --> 00:06:37,883
Good rotation means all your wheels reach this point together.

87
00:06:37,883 --> 00:06:41,534
That is far cheaper than replacing them one at a time.

88
00:06:41,534 --> 00:06:47,755
When that day comes, replace the whole set, so your diameters stay matched across the frame.

89
00:06:47,755 --> 00:06:57,221
And if you ever have to mix worn and new wheels, put the newest ones in the hardest wearing positions, and keep an eye on how they even out.

90
00:06:57,921 --> 00:06:59,613
Here is the short version.

91
00:06:59,613 --> 00:07:02,022
Feel your wheels after every session.

92
00:07:02,022 --> 00:07:06,513
Rotate every one to three sessions, and always flip them when you do.

93
00:07:06,513 --> 00:07:11,135
Keep the set matched, and check your bearings while the wheels are off.

94
00:07:11,135 --> 00:07:13,153
Store your skates cool and dry.

95
00:07:13,153 --> 00:07:19,728
Do that, and your wheels will stay faster, last far longer, and give you a better ride every session.

96
00:07:19,728 --> 00:07:26,237
The full guide, with all three diagrams and the frequently asked questions, is on the LiveLica blog.

97
00:07:26,237 --> 00:07:28,972
You will find the link in the description.
